#! beatscript 2
#! genre: https://ontology.bitwrap.io/genre/techno
#! tags: acid, supersaw, four on floor, drop
# linked.beatscript: a program that says what it is. The two lines above are header lines: comments to the language, so
# this runs anywhere and its CID is the same with or without them. A host that has the bitwrap ontology
# (https://ontology.bitwrap.io/ns#) reads them: `genre` is one genre (an IRI, or `techno`, `genre:techno`, `bw:Techno`),
# `tags` are terms of the vocabulary by local name, label or IRI (an instrument, a groove, a section ...). A misspelt
# one is a warning in `btw check` ("unknown tag `acidd`: did you mean `acid`?") and an error when it is saved.
# `btw jsonld` turns the program into linked data: its genre and tags, key, tempo, sections, licence, and the Euclidean
# rhythms below linked to the ontology's Euclidean rhythm class (e(4,16) is the shape of techno's own kick).
tempo 128
key am

section intro 8 bars {
  deck A: kick e(4,16) | hihat e(5,8)
}

section drop 16 bars {
  deck A: kick e(4,16) | snare e(2,16,4) | hihat e(5,8)
  deck B: bass <a1 ~ c2 ~> | harmony chord(am)
}

arrange intro drop
